# Generate/regenerate deliberately, on its own PR, exactly like requirements.txt:
#   pip install pip-tools && pip-compile requirements-dev.in
# (produces requirements-dev.txt). Prefer a Python 3.12 interpreter when regenerating, to
# match the Dockerfile/CI target exactly -- requirements-dev.txt in this repository was
# compiled under Python 3.13 (no 3.12 interpreter was available on the machine that
# generated it) since these are pure-Python CI tools with no 3.12-only wheel dependency;
# CI's own `pip install -r requirements-dev.txt` on the pinned python:3.12-slim image is
# the real verification -- if a version here is ever incompatible with 3.12, that install
# step fails loudly rather than silently succeeding with a lower-fidelity resolution.
#
# CI / local-development tooling only. Never installed into the production Docker image
# (Dockerfile installs requirements.txt exclusively) and never a runtime dependency of the
# application itself -- see .gitlab-ci.yml's `static-analysis`/`security` stages and
# scripts/ci/verify.py, which are the only things that actually import/exec these tools.
#
# Constrained against requirements.txt (-c, not -r): dev tooling must never silently pull a
# different version of a package requirements.txt already pins for the application itself
# (e.g. packaging, pydantic, httpx) -- pip-compile resolves against that pin, it does not
# additionally install requirements.txt's own packages into this file.
-c requirements.txt

